## Answer to the criteria: **No — not production ready.** Marker not written.

28 defects remain open (17 critical), and `open_defects` correctly FAILs the board.

**What this session did.** sess128 had written the BAST dispatch queue but never wired it — none of it executed. I completed the lifecycle wiring, built 0.11.453, deployed to all 32 nodes, and measured it.

**Wiring** (`dlm/dlm_caw.{c,h}`): `caw_bastq_init` in create; `caw_bastq_free` in both unwind paths; dispatchers created in `start` *before* both producers; new `caw_bastq_join_workers()` called in stop PHASE 3 *after* both producers and on both start-unwind paths. That join order is not stylistic — the PAL cond destroy is a bare `kfree` (`pal/linux/kern.c:1943`), so a worker still in `schedule_timeout` on a freed waitqueue is a UAF. Also added probe `P265-BASTQ-STATS` and harness `tests/bastq_snap.sh`.

**Result — 3/3 on the ledger's own deterministic recipe** (`prep_cluster` fresh mkfs → `crash_consistency`, which reproduced the FAIL 3/3 on 0.11.452):

| cycle | crash_consistency (virgin fs) | dRcvbufErrors / 32 nodes | dInDatagrams |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | PASS 32/32, 79s/90s | **0** | 624,089 |
| 2 | PASS 32/32, 79s/90s | **0** | 591,339 |
| 3 | PASS 32/32, 77s/90s | **0** | 573,471 |

Was FAIL 0/32, `NO_TERMINAL_RECORD=32`, 90s/90s, RcvbufErrors=8085. Full board re-run on the new build — every test, since BAST callbacks now run on new threads — is **27 PASS, 0 FAIL**, no regression.

**One finding that corrects the design's own premise:** coalescing is **1.05x**, with `hiwater=1`. Merging carries ~6% of the load; the dispatchers keep up completely. The fix does *not* work by deduplicating hints — it works by decoupling the UDP socket drain from the synchronous shared-LUN read, which is precisely the sess127 root, and RcvbufErrors 8085→0 is the direct evidence. A future session should not tune the coalescing table expecting it to matter.

**Next session starts here.** D-CRASH-CONSISTENCY-32-NOTERMINAL-354's stated closure condition is now met 3/3 but I did not get to updating the ledger entry — that disposition is the immediate next step. D-32NODE-SHARED-DIR-CREATE-PACE has 2 of 5 PASS-bar items met; three remain unmeasured (`caw_wait_for_grant` share, ino-128 grant wait, releases/run) plus its headline ~42x create-pace curve. Caveat captured in memory: `cc_grantwait.sh arm` sets `instr=1`, which that entry warns pushes an 86s run past 240s — it needs its own dedicated cycle, not a board run.

